     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi,</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks for your reply. I wanted to give this more extreme example too.  Here, there are ~22k loci and although the pvalues cover the range of 0 -1, the q-values are all 0.0054 (see screenshots below).  This doesn&#39;t make sense to me how a qvalue would be &lt;0.01 with a p value of 1.  Here, if slim=FALSE all of the q values = 1.  You mentioned the method expects a certain distribution of p values - what type of distribution is it? A normal distribution or something else? --Thanks for your help.</div><div><br></div><a href=\"http://eagle.fish.washington.edu/bivalvia/skitch/Output1_%5BDocument1%5D_-_IBM_SPSS_Statistics_Viewer-20140414-121159.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://eagle.fish.washington.edu/bivalvia/skitch/Output1_%255BDocument1%255D_-_IBM_SPSS_Statistics_Viewer-20140414-121159.jpg&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793234564898000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1tB2qrIIPXfQj2brmERndq\">p value screenshot</a><div><br></div><div><a href=\"http://eagle.fish.washington.edu/bivalvia/skitch/Output1_%5BDocument1%5D_-_IBM_SPSS_Statistics_Viewer-20140414-121257.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://eagle.fish.washington.edu/bivalvia/skitch/Output1_%255BDocument1%255D_-_IBM_SPSS_Statistics_Viewer-20140414-121257.jpg&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793234564898000&amp;usg=AOvVaw29k0jyhNPXwHk0x79sKpPf\">q value screenshoot</a> (the screenshot is rather silly since all 22k=0.0054)</div></div>"
